WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.201 --> 00:00:02.635
-Our next guest
is a very talented actor

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:04.037
and comedian you know
from shows such as

00:00:04.037 --> 00:00:06.105
"The Marvelous Mrs. Maisel"
and "Search Party."

00:00:06.105 --> 00:00:08.141
His podcast, "Dead Eyes,"
is available

00:00:08.141 --> 00:00:09.475
wherever you listen to podcasts.

00:00:09.475 --> 00:00:11.911
Please welcome to the show
Connor Ratliff, everybody!

00:00:11.911 --> 00:00:13.780
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:18.885 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:18.885 --> 00:00:23.956 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:23.956 --> 00:00:25.591
Hello, Connor! How are you?
-I'm great.

00:00:25.591 --> 00:00:27.060
How are you doing?
-I'm doing good.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.161
We have been
in the same room often.

00:00:29.161 --> 00:00:31.264
You've actually been in sketches
on this show

00:00:31.264 --> 00:00:32.765
that we filmed
outside of the studio.

00:00:32.765 --> 00:00:34.400
But this is the first time
we're meeting,

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:35.834
and I'm very excited about that.

00:00:35.834 --> 00:00:37.337
-I'm very excited, too.
It's very nice to meet you.

00:00:37.337 --> 00:00:39.972
-But I have also -- You have
a talk show of your own.

00:00:39.972 --> 00:00:42.508
-I do. I do a show called
"The George Lucas Talk Show."

00:00:42.508 --> 00:00:44.043
-Yep.
-I pretend to be retired

00:00:44.043 --> 00:00:46.078
filmmaker George Lucas
and interview real people

00:00:46.078 --> 00:00:48.782
as themselves, yeah.

00:00:48.782 --> 00:00:50.350
-And this has been something

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:51.851 line:0%
that's been shared
with me a lot.

00:00:51.851 --> 00:00:53.953 line:0%
You have a pillow,
a "Late Night with Seth Meyers"

00:00:53.953 --> 00:00:55.555 line:0%
pillow on the set of the --

00:00:55.555 --> 00:00:57.089 line:0%
-Yeah.
-There you are as George Lucas.

00:00:57.089 --> 00:00:59.559 line:0%
-Yeah.
-Why do you have my pillow?

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:02.128 line:0%
-You were doing South by
Southwest, and I was also there.

00:01:02.128 --> 00:01:03.696
And we were --
I was starting off the show

00:01:03.696 --> 00:01:05.098
around the same time.

00:01:05.098 --> 00:01:06.732
And I had lots of --
The set was decorated

00:01:06.732 --> 00:01:08.868
with talk-show paraphernalia
from other talk shows

00:01:08.868 --> 00:01:10.603
to establish that "George Lucas"

00:01:10.603 --> 00:01:13.540
was among the pantheon
of talk-show hosts.

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:16.543
And so we stole
one of your pillows.

00:01:16.543 --> 00:01:18.177
-Yeah.
-You had this display that had

00:01:18.177 --> 00:01:21.414
all these pillows at South by
Southwest, and you had so many

00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:24.049
that we thought,
"They won't miss one."

00:01:24.049 --> 00:01:26.386
And I actually thought,
you know,

00:01:26.386 --> 00:01:28.788
probably these other pillows
will end up in a landfill,

00:01:28.788 --> 00:01:32.124
and we will actually protect
this Seth by Southwest pillow.

00:01:32.124 --> 00:01:34.693
-I think that's probably
the most likely outcome.

00:01:34.693 --> 00:01:36.529
In the end, I don't know
where any of those pillows are,

00:01:36.529 --> 00:01:37.764
but it's a huge part
of your show.

00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:39.566
-Yeah, and the deal
has always been

00:01:39.566 --> 00:01:42.001
anytime we've had writers
from your show as guests

00:01:42.001 --> 00:01:44.270
on "The George Lucas Talk Show,"
we always say, "Tell Seth

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:46.139
if he wants his pillow..." --
Or George says it.

00:01:46.139 --> 00:01:48.007
George says, "Tell Seth
if he wants his pillow,

00:01:48.007 --> 00:01:49.208
he has to come on the show."

00:01:49.208 --> 00:01:50.710
-Yeah.
-And the offer stands.

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:52.645
-I'm very excited
to get the pillow back.

00:01:52.645 --> 00:01:54.147
-Yeah.
-I will be doing that.

00:01:54.147 --> 00:01:57.316
You -- This is, I think,
very common in podcasts now.

00:01:57.316 --> 00:01:58.785
It tries to solve a mystery.
-Yeah.

00:01:58.785 --> 00:02:00.987
-But unlike
other podcast mysteries,

00:02:00.987 --> 00:02:03.923
this is a very small,
tiny mystery.

00:02:03.923 --> 00:02:05.658
-Yeah.
-And this is "Dead Eyes,"

00:02:05.658 --> 00:02:07.593
and this is a story
that I had heard

00:02:07.593 --> 00:02:10.930
before I knew much about you.

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:12.331
And tell people
what the mystery is.

00:02:12.331 --> 00:02:14.066
-Okay.
So, in the year 2000,

00:02:14.066 --> 00:02:17.136
I was a young actor
fresh out of drama school.

00:02:17.136 --> 00:02:19.405
I was cast in a very small
speaking role

00:02:19.405 --> 00:02:21.507
in the HBO miniseries
"Band of Brothers."

00:02:21.507 --> 00:02:23.209
-A fantastic show.
-Great show. Great show.

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:24.644
-A fantastic show.
-I was so excited

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:26.212
to be part of it,
and then I found out,

00:02:26.212 --> 00:02:28.848
"Great news. Tom Hanks
is directing your episode."

00:02:28.848 --> 00:02:31.383
And then the day before I was
supposed to film my episode,

00:02:31.383 --> 00:02:33.919
I got a call, frantic,
from my agent's office saying,

00:02:33.919 --> 00:02:35.555
"You have to get there
right away.

00:02:35.555 --> 00:02:37.657
Tom Hanks has looked
at your audition tape

00:02:37.657 --> 00:02:39.392
and he's having second thoughts.

00:02:39.392 --> 00:02:42.061
He thinks you have dead eyes."

00:02:42.061 --> 00:02:44.230
And so I had to go
and re-audition

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:46.132
in person for Tom Hanks.

00:02:46.132 --> 00:02:47.467
And minutes later,

00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:50.503
I was told they were deciding
to go another way.

00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:52.037
-So this is -- Yes.
-[ Audience awws ]

00:02:52.037 --> 00:02:54.274
-A very sad story.
-Very sad story.

00:02:54.274 --> 00:02:57.110
-Yeah.
-Not only -- I think any actor

00:02:57.110 --> 00:03:00.346
would dread being told
they have dead eyes,

00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:02.348
but to get it from the person

00:03:02.348 --> 00:03:05.451
who is considered the nicest man
in show business.

00:03:05.451 --> 00:03:07.519
-Yes.
Like, if I had been cast

00:03:07.519 --> 00:03:09.255
in a very small role
in "Eyes Wide Shut"

00:03:09.255 --> 00:03:10.890
and then Stanley Kubrick
had said it,

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:12.425
I would have thought,
"Of course."

00:03:12.425 --> 00:03:14.394
-Yeah.
-You know?

00:03:14.394 --> 00:03:16.428
But this was the nicest man
in show business,

00:03:16.428 --> 00:03:19.098
so I thought,
"Oh, I'm so broken as a person.

00:03:19.098 --> 00:03:22.868
My eyes are so awful that
the nicest man in show business

00:03:22.868 --> 00:03:25.504
couldn't even bear to have me
be the tiniest part

00:03:25.504 --> 00:03:26.973
of 'Band of Brothers.'"

00:03:26.973 --> 00:03:28.608
"With all the horrible things
that happened in World War II,

00:03:28.608 --> 00:03:31.043
we can't show this
to the audience."

00:03:31.043 --> 00:03:33.646
-[ Laughter ]
-Now, had you ever even thought

00:03:33.646 --> 00:03:35.548
about your eyes
as being a detriment?

00:03:35.548 --> 00:03:38.384
-No, I had plenty of bad things
I thought about myself,

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:39.452
but the eyes, I thought, were,

00:03:39.452 --> 00:03:42.121
like, just fine, normal
human eyes.

00:03:42.121 --> 00:03:44.290
-The eyes were great.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:45.725
And since, I've heard nothing.

00:03:45.725 --> 00:03:47.793
It was the only time
I got that note.

00:03:47.793 --> 00:03:50.462
-And so you decided
to do this podcast

00:03:50.462 --> 00:03:52.531
to drill down because --

00:03:52.531 --> 00:03:55.668
This was a very --
I mean, all jokes aside,

00:03:55.668 --> 00:03:57.670
this was a very real thing
that affected you

00:03:57.670 --> 00:03:59.939
and affected your relationship
with acting.

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:02.074
-Yeah, I mean,
if it hadn't been this,

00:04:02.074 --> 00:04:04.143
it probably would've been
10 other things

00:04:04.143 --> 00:04:06.011
that would've added up
to me deciding,

00:04:06.011 --> 00:04:08.380
"I don't want to be an actor.
This feels terrible."

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:12.919
But I got it in one concentrated
dose of like -- Choonk!

00:04:12.919 --> 00:04:16.989
You know, "You are broken
and cannot do this."

00:04:16.989 --> 00:04:19.124
But I decided, you know --
I had never --

00:04:19.124 --> 00:04:22.194
He didn't say it to me. It was
a message I got through...

00:04:22.194 --> 00:04:24.630
-And, by the way,
I would say a better agent

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:26.665
would not have passed it
along to you like that.

00:04:26.665 --> 00:04:29.235
-It was a complete breakdown
along every part

00:04:29.235 --> 00:04:30.969
of the chain of communication.
-Yeah.

00:04:30.969 --> 00:04:33.872
-I never should've been told,
"Here's what's going on."

00:04:33.872 --> 00:04:35.207
They should've just said,
"You have to go in

00:04:35.207 --> 00:04:36.842
and read the scene again.
He wants to meet you."

00:04:36.842 --> 00:04:38.411
I would have been thrilled,
you know?

00:04:38.411 --> 00:04:43.116
-You have a guest coming up
on the show that I would love

00:04:43.116 --> 00:04:44.883
for you to announce now.
-Yes.

00:04:44.883 --> 00:04:48.387
So, on March 10th,
for our 31st episode,

00:04:48.387 --> 00:04:52.358
our season-3 finale will feature
my conversation with Tom Hanks.

00:04:52.358 --> 00:04:54.693
-Now, this is thrilling.
-Yeah.

00:04:54.693 --> 00:04:58.564
-This is thrilling.
-It's -- It's beyond thrilling.

00:04:58.564 --> 00:05:03.402
It's -- It is, you know --
I didn't expect it to happen.

00:05:03.402 --> 00:05:06.572
The podcast was designed -- The
podcast is about disappointment,

00:05:06.572 --> 00:05:09.374
so I would be foolish --
It was baked into the DNA

00:05:09.374 --> 00:05:11.678
of the podcast that maybe
we'll never get to him.

00:05:11.678 --> 00:05:14.880
-Right.
-But we did. And it's great.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:19.184
It's a -- We have a fully --
we hash out everything,

00:05:19.184 --> 00:05:24.023
and it's everything that
I wanted from the conversation.

00:05:24.023 --> 00:05:25.824
I would not have gotten
this conversation

00:05:25.824 --> 00:05:27.926
if I had played the role.
-Right.

00:05:27.926 --> 00:05:29.228
-There would never be a world
in which

00:05:29.228 --> 00:05:32.531
I played this small role
and then 22 years later

00:05:32.531 --> 00:05:34.499
I got to talk to Tom Hanks
for over an hour.

00:05:34.499 --> 00:05:36.101
-Right.
-You know? So it's --

00:05:36.101 --> 00:05:38.370
-Yeah, the guy who got
that part right now is like,

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:40.472
"What the [bleep]?"

00:05:40.472 --> 00:05:43.642
"Here I am over there.
I got this light in my eyes."

00:05:43.642 --> 00:05:45.210
-He's doing great.
He actually does --

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:47.246
He has a great career.
He does a lot of audio books.

00:05:47.246 --> 00:05:48.714
He's doing fine.
-Alright.

00:05:48.714 --> 00:05:50.950
I like that you've kept up
with him, as well.

00:05:50.950 --> 00:05:52.351
-I have. He's a very --

00:05:52.351 --> 00:05:54.119
I talked to him on the podcast.
A very nice guy.

00:05:54.119 --> 00:05:55.888
He had no idea it had happened.

00:05:55.888 --> 00:05:59.024
I actually found out when I
talked to him that the same day

00:05:59.024 --> 00:06:00.359
that I was re-auditioning,

00:06:00.359 --> 00:06:03.428
there was a second car
filled with alternate actors

00:06:03.428 --> 00:06:06.965
who were ready to take my place
if and when I got fired.

00:06:06.965 --> 00:06:08.701
-[ Laughs ] Oh, my God.
-Yeah.

00:06:08.701 --> 00:06:11.070
-And now, obviously,
now you're doing the podcast,

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:12.438
so it comes up all the time

00:06:12.438 --> 00:06:13.805
that you think
about this moment.

00:06:13.805 --> 00:06:16.008
-Yeah.
-World events right now

00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:18.043
might also make you think
about the part.

00:06:18.043 --> 00:06:21.180
-Yeah, the character's name
was Private Zielinski.

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:25.951
And if you follow the news,
you might have heard that name

00:06:25.951 --> 00:06:28.787
almost every minute
of every day now,

00:06:28.787 --> 00:06:31.023
one of the most famous names
in the world right now.

00:06:31.023 --> 00:06:32.191
-Right now.

00:06:32.191 --> 00:06:34.426
The other thing
that is amazing to me,

00:06:34.426 --> 00:06:36.295
even more amazing
than the fact that you got

00:06:36.295 --> 00:06:38.097
Tom Hanks to do
your podcast, we --

00:06:38.097 --> 00:06:42.568
Our research did some digging,
and you as a young man were --

00:06:42.568 --> 00:06:44.636
You went to sort of a C-SPAN --
What was it?

00:06:44.636 --> 00:06:46.305
-I was on, like,
a high school --

00:06:46.305 --> 00:06:48.240
It was the senior year
of high school, went on a trip,

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:49.742
and I guess that's how C-SPAN

00:06:49.742 --> 00:06:51.176
gets a lot of their
studio audiences.

00:06:51.176 --> 00:06:53.512
It's just, like,
gangs of students.

00:06:53.512 --> 00:06:55.648
They just go in here
and watch a C-SPAN taping.

00:06:55.648 --> 00:06:58.317
And it was a debate
about Russia and Ukraine.

00:06:58.317 --> 00:06:59.785
-So, here you are.

00:06:59.785 --> 00:07:03.856 line:0%
There you are on C-SPAN in 1992,
I guess, maybe?

00:07:03.856 --> 00:07:05.624 line:0%
-Yeah.
Yeah, end of '92, I think.

00:07:05.624 --> 00:07:08.727 line:0%
And I was determined
to get on camera,

00:07:08.727 --> 00:07:10.629
so I asked a question
about the differences

00:07:10.629 --> 00:07:13.165
culturally between
Russia and Ukraine.

00:07:13.165 --> 00:07:14.767
And the Russian person,
she said,

00:07:14.767 --> 00:07:16.502
"Oh, there's really
no differences."

00:07:16.502 --> 00:07:18.404
We're all the same.
We're all one thing."

00:07:18.404 --> 00:07:20.406
And the Ukrainian representative
was like,

00:07:20.406 --> 00:07:21.873
"No, we are different."

00:07:21.873 --> 00:07:23.709
And, I mean, you can see.

00:07:23.709 --> 00:07:25.644
It's just, like,
such a call forward to, like,

00:07:25.644 --> 00:07:27.713
"Well, this is not
gonna end well."

00:07:27.713 --> 00:07:30.149
-[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:07:30.149 --> 00:07:32.885
-This was also --
It should be noted --

00:07:32.885 --> 00:07:35.421
You're doing this podcast now,
but was this a story

00:07:35.421 --> 00:07:38.490
that you told people
before you did the podcast?

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:41.227
-Yeah, I mean, for a while,
it was an embarrassing story

00:07:41.227 --> 00:07:43.429
that I had to tell people
because I told everybody

00:07:43.429 --> 00:07:44.763
that I was going to be
on "Band of Brothers."

00:07:44.763 --> 00:07:46.765
So it was kind of like --
I would tell it reluctantly.

00:07:46.765 --> 00:07:48.200
Like, "Why aren't you on
'Band of Brothers'?"

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:49.568
"Well, here's why."

00:07:49.568 --> 00:07:51.336
Then when I started doing,
like, comedy at UCB

00:07:51.336 --> 00:07:52.638
and I started doing
improv and stuff,

00:07:52.638 --> 00:07:54.172
people would be like,
"So, what's your story?"

00:07:54.172 --> 00:07:55.707
And I was like,
"Well, I used to be an actor,

00:07:55.707 --> 00:07:57.843
and now I'm doing this."
And they're like, "Why?"

00:07:57.843 --> 00:07:59.845
You know, I would tell people
within the first

00:07:59.845 --> 00:08:01.347
10 minutes of meeting them,
you know?

00:08:01.347 --> 00:08:03.549
Like, I'm finding out
as I do the podcast --

00:08:03.549 --> 00:08:05.551
I would ask friends, "When did
I tell you this story?"

00:08:05.551 --> 00:08:07.720
And they'd be like,
"Five minutes after we met."

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:09.689
-[ Laughter ]
-Because it just explained,

00:08:09.689 --> 00:08:12.424
like, well, here's why I'm doing
improv in a basement for free,

00:08:12.424 --> 00:08:14.626
because this is fun
and I don't like show business.

00:08:14.626 --> 00:08:18.931
-And, also, I will say,
it's -- if it wasn't real,

00:08:18.931 --> 00:08:20.265
it would be the greatest

00:08:20.265 --> 00:08:22.134
beginning of an improv scene
of all time.

00:08:22.134 --> 00:08:23.369
-Oh, absolutely.

00:08:23.369 --> 00:08:25.070
Yeah, "Tom Hanks thinks
you have dead eyes"

00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:28.207
is a great initiation to the --
The scene writes itself.

00:08:28.207 --> 00:08:29.675
-The scene writes itself.
-Yeah.

00:08:29.675 --> 00:08:31.910
-And I know the podcast
hasn't written itself,

00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:33.612
and it's been
a lot of hard work.

00:08:33.612 --> 00:08:36.248
But, man, oh, man, has it been
a fantastic journey

00:08:36.248 --> 00:08:38.717
to listen to you talk about it,
and I cannot wait to hear

00:08:38.717 --> 00:08:40.452
the Tom Hanks episode.
-It's gonna be great.

00:08:40.452 --> 00:08:42.555
Thank you so much.
-Alright. That's Connor Ratliff.

00:08:42.555 --> 00:08:46.058
Subscribe to "Dead Eyes"
wherever you listen to podcasts.

